# Manuscript PDF page 78

                         HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ




Mamá stopped me in the kitchen on Saturday with a covered Pyrex in
both hands.
    “Llévale esto a Luz.”
    “Má, she doesn’t—”
    “Two years, I leave food at that door. Two years, the dish comes back
washed. Not one word.” She pushed the Pyrex into my chest. “In this
building a Pyrex is a diplomatic cable. Take it. And bring back the dish.
Don’t make me come get it.”
    I stood on the landing with sixteen pounds of baked macaroni and
looked at the wreath for a full minute.
    I knocked. Two flat knocks. It’s the only knock I have.
    It took a long time. Then a chain, and a bolt, and another bolt, and
the door opened the width of a woman.
    Doña Luz is small, sixty, fierce, in a house dress and flip-flops, with
her hair in a net and a look that has outlasted a lot of men who thought
they could wait her out. She looked at me. She looked at the Pyrex. I
watched her decide, one second at a time, whether I was a cousin of the
thing that had taken her son or just another neighbor with macaroni.
    “Tu mamá cocina demasiado,” she said, and took the dish, and
stood aside.
    The apartment was my apartment with the colors changed. Same
layout, same radiator, same window onto the El. Saints on every flat
surface, plastic on the couch, the TV on mute with a telenovela having
a bad day. On top of the fridge, wrapped in a dish towel and tied with
kitchen string like a small body, was a white speaker with a camera-iris
decal on it.
    I looked at it, and she watched me look.
    “Se lo dio Mikey,” she said. “A gift. I put it up there.” Her mouth
was a flat line. “I didn’t turn it on. I didn’t throw it out either. Throwing
it out would’ve been saying something.”
    She sat me at the kitchen table. She didn’t offer coffee, which in our
culture is a declaration of war. She sat across from me with her hands
folded.



